{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n`choosy-arthrodic-barbet` removed compaction from the close path because it held the ONE store\nwrite lock for up to 13m53s, starving concurrent agents. That landed the P0 fix but left the\nstore with no standing compaction trigger: event logs grow until someone runs\n`rebar compact <id>` by hand. This ticket restores an automatic trigger that never touches an\ninteractive session's store lock.\n\n## Approach\nTwo small pieces, both reusing what already exists.\n\n**1. Make `compact-all` recur.** The sweep is nearly built: `compact_all_cli`\n(`src/rebar/_commands/compact.py:213`) already enumerates ticket dirs, honours\n`--dry-run` / `--limit` / `--no-commit`, folds each ticket through `compact_cli`, batches ONE\ncommit + push, and reports counts. Its only defect here is SELECTION: `_scan_snapshot_state`\n(`src/rebar/_commands/compact.py:144`) splits tickets into \"lacking a `-SNAPSHOT.json`\" and\n\"already has one\", and compacts only the former. A ticket folded once that has since\naccumulated hundreds of live events counts as `already` and is never folded again — so\n`compact-all` is a one-time BACKFILL, not a recurring sweep.\n\nFix the selection, not the driver: select a ticket on EITHER of two arms — (1) BACKFILL, the\nhistorical arm preserved verbatim: no `-SNAPSHOT.json` and at least one foldable event; or\n(2) RECURRENCE, the new arm: foldable event count over `compact.threshold`\n(`src/rebar/_commands/compact.py:81`), whatever its snapshot state. Arm 2 alone would regress\nbackfill (a ticket with fewer events than the threshold would never earn a first SNAPSHOT);\narm 1 alone is the one-time-only defect above. Both converge: after a fold the ticket has a\nSNAPSHOT and its live count is back under threshold, so neither arm re-selects it. No new\nmodule, no second fold engine: the snapshot semantics, horizon partitioning and authorship\nledger in `compact_txn` are untouched.\n\nFOLDABLE, not merely live, is load-bearing. The fold squashes only events older than the\ncompaction horizon (`_compact_locked` partitions on `is_foldable`,\n`src/rebar/_commands/compact_txn.py:365`; horizon defaults to 30 min,\n`src/rebar/_config_schema.py:332`). Selecting on the RAW live count would pick a ticket whose\nexcess events are all inside the horizon; the fold would write nothing, the driver would count\nit compacted, and the next sweep would pick it again — churn plus a lying count. So selection\nreuses the fold's OWN predicate, `is_foldable` (`src/rebar/_commands/_compact_policy.py`),\nagainst the same horizon, so the two cannot drift.\nThe driver must also report honestly, and cannot do so from the return code: `_compact_locked`\nreturns 0 for a successful fold AND for every no-op branch — below threshold\n(`compact_txn.py:344`), nothing older than the horizon (`compact_txn.py:369`), no safe\nplacement gap (`compact_txn.py:383`) — so `compact_all_cli`'s `if rc == 0: compacted += 1`\n(`src/rebar/_commands/compact.py:255`) counts all of them as work. Rather than add a return\ncode (a contract change every `compact_cli` caller inherits), the driver observes the\nARTIFACT: record the `*-SNAPSHOT.json` names in the ticket dir before and after the fold, and\ncount it compacted only when a new one appeared. Uniform across all three no-op branches, no\ncontract change, and the same observable-artifact oracle the barbet tests use.\n\n**2. Trigger it out of band every 6 hours** (`cron: '0 */6 * * *'`), reusing the reconciler's\nproven recipe rather\nthan inventing a clone concept. `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml` already runs a\nscheduled job that WRITES ticket events and pushes them to the `tickets` branch, from an\nephemeral runner that shares nothing with any interactive checkout. The new workflow copies\nits store-mount and identity steps verbatim in shape.\n\n*Clone lifecycle* — there is no long-lived clone to manage, which is the point:\n- **Create:** `actions/checkout` gives a fresh clone per run; the store is mounted with the\n  reconciler's exact steps (`reconcile-bridge.yml:126-130`) — full (non-shallow)\n  `git fetch origin '+tickets:refs/remotes/origin/tickets'`, `rm -rf .tickets-tracker`,\n  `git worktree add -B tickets .tickets-tracker origin/tickets`.\n- **Refresh:** each run re-derives the store from `origin/tickets`, so no stale state carries\n  between runs. The tickets merge driver is configured (`reconcile-bridge.yml:132-134`) so the\n  push-retry loop can merge `origin/tickets` when the branch moved mid-run.\n- **Retire:** the runner is disposable. A failed push leaves local commits on a workspace that\n  is discarded; the next run starts from `origin/tickets` and re-folds whatever is still over\n  threshold. Nothing accumulates, so there is no stale-clone cleanup class.\n\n*Signing and identity* — both resolve without bootstrapping anything new:\n- The bot git identity is configured as the reconciler does (`reconcile-bridge.yml:215-222`).\n- `secrets.REBAR_BOT_SIGNING_KEY` is materialized to a 0600 file and exported as\n  `REBAR_IDENTITY_SIGNING_KEY` (`reconcile-bridge.yml:233-248`); the seam signs when that key\n  resolves and writes unsigned (advisory) when it does not — the reconciler's posture today.\n- Environment identity needs NO bootstrap: `_seam.env_id`\n  (`src/rebar/_commands/_seam.py:155`) reads `<tracker>/.env-id`, which is CONTENT of the\n  tickets branch, so a worktree of `origin/tickets` carries the store's own env id\n  automatically.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_commands/compact.py` — threshold-based selection so `compact-all` recurs.\n- `.github/workflows/compact-sweep.yml` — new scheduled workflow (store mount + identity +\n  key materialization modeled on `reconcile-bridge.yml`), with `workflow_dispatch`.\n- `docs/concurrency.md` — record the 6-hourly trigger under the I9b section barbet added.\n- `tests/unit/test_compact_all_selection.py` — new selection tests.\n- `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_compact_sweep_races.py` — new convergence tests.\n\n## Testing\n- Selection: a ticket WITH a SNAPSHOT plus FOLDABLE events over threshold is selected; one\n  whose excess events are all inside the horizon is NOT (the churn case); one under threshold\n  is not; one with no SNAPSHOT (the backfill case) still is.\n- Double-snapshot race: two sweeps fold the same ticket from separate clones and both push;\n  the merged tracker reduces to the same state with no duplicate or lost events.\n- Concurrent tail: events appended while a fold runs replay on top of the SNAPSHOT.\n- Clone signing: a fold run from a fresh worktree of the tickets branch produces the same\n  authorship ledger and event signatures as an in-place fold.\n- Failure path: a rejected push leaves the local commits intact and reports the failure.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `compact-all` selects an already-snapshotted ticket whose FOLDABLE event count exceeds\n      the threshold — a test seeds one, runs the sweep, and asserts it was folded.\n- [ ] The sweep converges: a ticket over threshold whose excess events are all inside the\n      compaction horizon is not selected, and if selected is not counted as compacted — a test\n      runs two consecutive sweeps and asserts the second reports zero compacted.\n- [ ] It still skips under-threshold tickets and still backfills snapshot-less ones — the same\n      test asserts both, so the change is a widening and not a replacement.\n- [ ] Two concurrent sweeps folding one ticket from separate clones converge — a test merges\n      both results and asserts identical reduced state with no duplicate or lost events.\n- [ ] Events appended during a fold survive it — a test asserts the tail replays on top of the\n      SNAPSHOT and appears in reduced state.\n- [ ] A fold run from a fresh tickets worktree signs identically to an in-place fold — a test\n      compares authorship ledger entries and event signatures.\n- [ ] The scheduled workflow exists with `cron: '0 */6 * * *'`, runs the sweep against a\n      mounted tickets worktree, and is `workflow_dispatch`-able; `docs/concurrency.md` records\n      that cadence, and the full local gate set plus CI's pytest tiers pass."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786607218093630001,"uuid":"f7a9d45f-768f-4b73-8dc0-8d3eed6f5b03"}